// =============================================================================
// Agriculture ERP -- Prisma schema
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PINNED TO PRISMA 6.19.3, DELIBERATELY
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Exact pins, no carets: "prisma": "6.19.3" and "@prisma/client": "6.19.3".
//
// This is a decision, not neglect. The pooled-plus-direct connection pattern
// below is the path Supabase documents, and this team cannot read code. A
// well-trodden path is worth more to us than being current.
//
// Prisma 7 removed `url` and `directUrl` from this datasource block and moved
// them to a prisma.config.ts plus a driver adapter. That is a real change to
// how the application connects, and it will be made once, deliberately, as its
// own unit with its own attack review -- never as a side effect of scaffolding.
//
// Do not bump either package as part of unrelated work.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `prisma migrate dev` IS NOT USED ON THIS PROJECT
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `migrate dev` requires a shadow database to diff against, and it creates that
// database itself. The Supabase `postgres` role cannot create databases, so the
// command times out or fails outright. That failure is expected. It is not a
// bug to chase, and it is not fixed by retrying.
//
// Migrations here are hand-written SQL in timestamped folders under
// prisma/migrations, applied with `prisma migrate deploy` (pnpm db:migrate).
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// PRISMA BYPASSES ROW-LEVEL SECURITY, BY DESIGN -- DO NOT "FIX" THIS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Prisma connects as the database owner and therefore bypasses RLS entirely.
// That is correct and intended.
//
// RLS is our backstop against the anon and authenticated Supabase keys, which
// reach the database directly from clients. It is NOT our defence against our
// own server. Server-side, authorisation is enforced by requireRole in the
// Next.js API routes, which checks session and role before any query runs.
//
// So: every table enables RLS with no policies (deny-by-default), and Prisma
// bypasses it as owner. Both are deliberate. A future session that "fixes" this
// by connecting Prisma as a restricted role will break every API route and gain
// nothing.
//
// Prisma cannot author RLS policies. No Prisma version has schema syntax for
// ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY or CREATE POLICY. Policies are hand-written SQL in
// migrations, which is why that rule is written into the migration files.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE TWO CONNECTION URLS
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//   DATABASE_URL  Pooled, port 6543, pgbouncer=true & connection_limit=1.
//                 Used by the application at runtime.
//
//   DIRECT_URL    Direct, port 5432. Used by MIGRATIONS and introspection,
//                 which cannot run through the pooler.
//
// Both carry ?schema=public. That is how Prisma is restricted to the public
// schema, so it never introspects, migrates or drops the auth, storage,
// realtime or graphql schemas that Supabase manages itself.
//
// Real values live in .env.local only, never in this repository.
// =============================================================================

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client-js"
}

datasource db {
  provider  = "postgresql"
  url       = env("DATABASE_URL")
  directUrl = env("DIRECT_URL")
}

/// Throwaway smoke-test table for unit B1.3. Proves a migration applies and
/// reverses. Created in migration 2, dropped in migration 3. Holds no real
/// data and is not part of the data model.
model Smoke {
  id   Int    @id @default(autoincrement())
  note String

  @@map("_smoke")
}
